---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/replenishment';
const title = "Replenishment email: estimate when a reorder is useful";
const desc = "Build reminders around product supply, quantity, delivery uncertainty and subscription state. Treat the consumption date as an estimate that customers can correct, not proof that a product has run out.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is thirty days a useful default?",
    "answer": "Only when it matches a defensible supply estimate for that product and customer context. Use a range and allow corrections instead of applying one interval to the whole catalog."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should every subscriber be excluded?",
    "answer": "Check whether the subscription covers the item being reminded about and whether a shipment is already scheduled. An unrelated subscription should not automatically block a useful reminder."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does an early reorder reset the clock?",
    "answer": "Re-evaluate the pending reminder. Additional quantity may extend supply, so define how the estimate changes rather than assuming the customer started using the new purchase immediately."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Workflow guide</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Create a supply table with an owner</h2>
      <p>Record product and variant ID, pack size, expected usage range, source of that assumption, delivery anchor and review date. Different vendor lines or reformulations may need separate rows even when they share a category.</p>
      <p>Use approved product instructions and customer research alongside order history. A repeat-purchase interval includes shipping, stockpiling, gifting and promotions; it does not directly measure depletion. Do not turn a purchase estimate into dosage advice or a claim about how much a household should consume.</p>
      <h2>Choose a useful reminder window</h2>
      <p>Start with a range that allows time to order and receive a replacement. Decide whether the clock starts at delivery, estimated receipt or another known usage event. Record uncertainty when the delivery event is absent.</p>
      <p>A reminder at a fixed percentage of supply is a hypothesis to compare, not a standard. Neutral copy such as checking whether a refill is needed avoids claiming knowledge of the cupboard. Let customers say not yet or stop reminders for the product.</p>
      <h2>Track product-level state</h2>
      <p>Keep the customer, product, relevant purchase, quantity and next eligible reminder together. Mixed orders can contain products with very different supply windows. Where item-level triggers are unavailable, consider a simpler family-level reminder with transparent wording rather than pretending to have item precision.</p>
      <p>A new purchase of the same product should re-evaluate pending reminders. It may add supply rather than mean immediate consumption restarted. Define how stockpiling and replacements affect the estimate so a resend or duplicate order event does not create another timer.</p>
      <h2>Handle bundles and household variation</h2>
      <p>Unpack bundles into refillable components when that is how customers buy replacements. Do not assume the first depleted component means the entire bundle is empty. Consolidate nearby reminders where a combined shopping task is useful.</p>
      <p>Quantity, shared use, multiple pets and occasional use can all change timing. Ask only for information that helps the reminder, explain its purpose and support an unknown value. Explicit gift orders should not inherit the same personal-consumption estimate as self-purchases.</p>
      <h2>Recheck subscription state before sending</h2>
      <p>Distinguish a subscription covering this product from any subscription on the account. A customer may subscribe to coffee while buying filters separately. Account for active, paused, cancelled and pending states, and a shipment already scheduled.</p>
      <p>Re-evaluate at send time where possible, not only when the customer enters the flow. If subscription data is stale, avoid a confident buy-another message until the state is reconciled. Subscribers need the appropriate account-management path, not a duplicate one-time purchase ask.</p>
      <h2>Keep the reorder destination accurate</h2>
      <p>Link to the intended variant or a clear selection page. Verify pack size, current price, stock, shipping market and any subscription choice. Do not silently replace a discontinued product with a different formula or size.</p>
      <p>For a temporary stockout, offer an honest availability path instead of continuing urgent reminders. Explain successor products and meaningful differences. Reorder links should not complete an unexpected purchase or subscription without the customer reviewing the terms.</p>
      <h2>Choose incentives from contribution economics</h2>
      <p>A convenience reminder can start without an incentive, but full-price buying is not a universal benchmark. Compare the store’s actual offer options and product costs. Explain subscription commitments and any advertised saving accurately.</p>
      <p>Measure whether an incentive changes total contribution across a suitable follow-up window. A one-time order and a subscription require comparable horizons, refunds, fulfillment costs and retention assumptions. Do not count an assumed lifetime of recurring payments as realized profit.</p>
      <h2>Coordinate replenishment with other journeys</h2>
      <p>Post-purchase education explains use; replenishment asks whether another supply is needed; winback addresses a longer purchase lapse. Define those boundaries per product family so a buyer with plenty of stock is not automatically treated as lost.</p>
      <p>Coordinate loyalty rewards and campaign offers so the reminder does not advertise incompatible terms. Resolve an active delivery or return problem before promoting another supply. Set frequency around the combined customer workload rather than an arbitrary monthly channel cap.</p>
      <h2>Calibrate against observable outcomes</h2>
      <p>Track reminder eligibility, sends, not-yet responses, reorders before and after reminders, subscription changes, returns and complaints by product family. Compare mature cohorts with equal observation windows. Include people who did not reorder instead of analyzing successful repeat buyers alone.</p>
      <p>Observed reorder timing can help revise assumptions but is not proof that a reminder caused an order. For a timing or incentive experiment, define comparable eligible groups and the success measure in advance. Watch for substitution between subscription and one-time orders, not merely growth in attributed flow revenue.</p>
      <h2>Demonstrate the exceptions before release</h2>
      <p>Request a demonstration of a mixed basket, extra quantity, early reorder, partial refund, subscription started while queued, stockout and changed pack size. Check event retries and a stale subscription feed. These are proposed checks, not results of a supplier trial.</p>
      <p>Compare timer maintenance, integration work, message charges and support time against improving the current stack. Release with a versioned supply table and a pause owner. Review assumptions after product changes and meaningful timing errors; no fixed migration season or universal payback claim is needed.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <nav class="container pb-12 flex flex-wrap gap-4" aria-label="Related workflows">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">Post-purchase →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases/loyalty">Loyalty →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases/winback">Winback →</a>
  </nav>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
